Probe stubs. Each fixture installs a `Pilot.probe` override wherever it builds a `Pilot`, because `command` never sees a probe call. Without the override, a real `probe` would run git in a temp directory that is not a repository, exit 128, and count as a sync error. The local ref each stub answers comes from a fixture dict `self.local_refs` (branch -> sha), never from a guess:
- a small helper `self.pull_at(pr)` sets `self.local_refs[pr['head']['ref']] = pr['head']['sha']` and then calls `self.pilot.pull(...)`; existing tests that pull switch to it, so every existing pull sees equal refs and no sync. Tests that need a lag set `self.local_refs` themselves and call `pull` directly.
- `rev-parse --verify --quiet refs/heads/<b>` answers `self.local_refs[b]`, or (1, '', '') when absent; the private-fetch `rev-parse refs/fm/fetch/…` answers the head of the PR being pulled, which the helper records in `self.fetch_head`; every other sync call answers per the list above.
- tests/lib/autopilot_cases.py `setUp` (:25-49): `self.pilot.probe = self.probe`, which appends the argv list to `self.calls`, the same shape `command` records (:52), so call-list assertions see the PUT.
- tests/lib/autopilot_loop.py `setUp` (:26-44): the override appends `('probe', argv)`, matching that file's `('command', argv)` tuples (:46). Its `command` stub (:46-50) is not changed, so `base_tip()` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot_loop.py:98-100) still answers BASE and `test_stale_head_or_base_never_cards` (:111-120) holds. `test_new_worker_head_after_reject_enters_gates` (:336-342) pulls heads `a…` then `c…` on one branch and PR number through `pull_at`, so the local ref follows each pull.
- tests/lib/merge_authorization.py `restart()` (:31-35): the override is installed there, so it survives each new `Pilot`; it answers only and records nothing.
- tests/lib/autopilot_task_resolution.py keeps its git stub on PATH (:31-53), so a real `probe` reaches it.
The PUT answer the stubs return for a 202 is rc 0, stderr '' and stdout `HTTP/2.0 202 Accepted\r\nContent-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8\r\n\r\n{"message":"Updating pull request branch.","url":"https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/12"}`. For the 'moved' 422 it is rc 1, stderr 'gh: Unprocessable Entity (HTTP 422)' and stdout `HTTP/2.0 422 Unprocessable Entity\r\nContent-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8\r\n\r\n{"message":"expected head sha didn't match current head ref.","documentation_url":"https://docs.github.com/rest/pulls/pulls#update-a-pull-request-branch","status":"422"}`. Other cases use the same layout with their own status line and body. For git calls the stubs return (rc, stdout, stderr): (1, '', '') for a missing branch, exit 0/1/128 for `merge-base --is-ancestor` (128 with stderr 'fatal: Not a valid commit name'), and the local ref from `self.local_refs` as above (for example `test_only_mergeable_behind…` at autopilot_cases.py:64-67 pulls heads `c…` and `d…` on one branch through `pull_at`).
